.TH SHOTSORT 1 "2026-09-12" "shotsort 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
shotsort \- sort photos and video
by the day they were shot
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shotsort
.RI [ OPTION ]...
.IR FOLDER ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shotsort
groups photos and video by the day they were taken, using the date the camera
recorded inside each file rather than anything the filesystem claims. It is
meant for the folder you get after copying a card: point it at that folder and
it replaces the camera's
.I DCIM
layout with one folder per shooting day.
.PP
Files are copied, never moved, unless
.B \-\-move
is given, so that a mistaken run costs nothing but disk space.
.PP
Every file is examined and its destination decided before anything is written,
so nothing moves until the whole plan is settled.
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints that plan.
.PP
A
.I FOLDER
must always be named:
.B shotsort
run with no arguments prints its options and sorts nothing, and it never falls
back to the folder you are working in. Day folders are created at the top level
of the folder you name, or of
.B \-\-dest
when that is given, and the files are copied into them. Under
.B \-\-move
the files are moved instead, and any folder the move leaves empty is removed.
Running the command a second time on the same folder does nothing.
.PP
Nothing is ever overwritten and no file is read from a card that is not
explicitly named. See
.B SAFETY
below.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-source " " \fIFOLDER\fR
The folder to sort. The same as naming it without a flag, and worth spelling
out whenever
.B \-\-dest
is used too, so it is plain which folder is read and which is written. May be
given more than once.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dest " " \fIFOLDER\fR
Put the day folders in
.I FOLDER
instead of inside the source folder. With copying left as the default, this
builds a sorted library and leaves the source exactly as it came off the card.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print the plan and change nothing. The plan shown is the one that would be
carried out, computed by the same code.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-move
Move the files rather than copying them, leaving each source folder empty and
then removing it. Without this the originals are left exactly where they are,
so the source folder ends up holding both them and the sorted copies unless
.B \-\-dest
sends the copies elsewhere.
.TP
.BR \-\-layout " " \fIFORMAT\fR
How to name each day folder.
.B %Y
is the year,
.B %m
the month,
.B %d
the day, and
.B %F
all three joined by dashes. A slash makes a subfolder, so
.B \-\-layout '%Y/%F'
gives
.IR 2026/2026\-08\-27 .
Must be a relative path holding at least one date escape. Default:
.BR %Y\-%m\-%d .
.TP
.BR \-\-day\-start " " \fIHOUR\fR
The hour at which one shooting day becomes the next. The default, 0, turns the
day at midnight, so a wedding shot from 20:00 on the 27th until 01:30 on the
28th is split between
.I 2026\-08\-27
and
.IR 2026\-08\-28 .
.B \-\-day\-start 4
turns the day at 04:00 instead, filing everything shot before 04:00 under the
previous day, so that whole night lands in
.I 2026\-08\-27
together. An hour from 0 to 12.
.TP
.B \-\-use\-filesystem\-date
Date every file that records no date inside itself by the date the filesystem
keeps, without checking that date first. See
.B HOW THE DATE IS FOUND.
.TP
.B \-\-ignore\-filesystem\-date
Date none of them that way: every file that records no date inside itself goes
to
.IR undated/ .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print every file as it is placed, as
.RI \(lq source " \-> " destination \(rq.
Files already in the right place are not printed.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print nothing but errors.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the plan and the result as JSON on standard output: every file with the
folder chosen for it, the clock the date came from, and what was done, and a
summary carrying everything the notes on standard error would have said:
how many files were dated by the filesystem, how many were left undated, and
how many names had to be split into numbered subfolders.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage text and exit, exactly as running
.B shotsort
with no arguments does.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.PP
Short options may be run together:
.B \-nv
is
.BR "\-n \-v" .
A
.B \-\-
argument ends option parsing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every file was placed.
.TP
.B 1
Some files were not placed, or none were found.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was wrong, naming no folder to sort included. Running
.B shotsort
with no arguments at all therefore prints the usage text and exits
.BR 2 .
.SH FILE TYPES
Stills:
.BR JPG ", " JPEG ", " JPE ", " JPS ", " MPO ", " INSP ", " PNG ", " APNG ", " WEBP ", " JXL ", " HSP ", " HIF ", " HEIC ", " HEIF ", " AVIF ", " THM .
.PP
Raw, by the maker that writes it:
.RS
.PD 0
.TP 20
Panasonic, Leica
.BR RW2 ", " RAW ", " RWL
.TP
Canon
.BR CR2 ", " CR3 ", " CRM ", " CRW
.TP
Fujifilm
.B RAF
.TP
Nikon
.BR NEF ", " NRW
.TP
Sony
.BR ARW ", " ARQ ", " SR2 ", " SRF
.TP
Minolta, Sigma
.BR MRW ", " X3F
.TP
Olympus, OM System
.BR ORF ", " ORI
.TP
Pentax, Ricoh
.B PEF
.TP
Others
.BR SRW ", " ERF ", " 3FR ", " FFF ", " IIQ ", " MOS ", " MEF ", " DCR ", " KDC ", " K25 ", " GPR ", " JXR ", " HDP ", " WDP
.TP
Anyone
.BR DNG ", " TIF ", " TIFF ", " BTF
.PD
.RE
.PP
Video:
.BR MP4 ", " MOV ", " QT ", " M4V ", " MQV ", " 3GP ", " 3GPP ", " 3G2 ", " 3GP2 ", " MTS ", " M2TS ", " M2T ", " AVI ", " MKV ", " WEBM ", " WMV ", " ASF ", " DIVX ", " MPG ", " MPEG ", " M2V ", " VOB ", " DV ", " F4V ", " R3D ", " LRV ", " LRF ", " GLV ", " INSV ", " 360 ,
including 4K and 6K PHOTO clips and the proxies action cameras and drones
write alongside them.
.PP
The extension decides only which files are picked up; which parser runs is
decided by the file's leading bytes, so a mislabelled file still reads
correctly. Most of those raws are a TIFF container and one parser reads them
all. The number after the byte order mark is not checked against a list of
makers: every maker who dressed a raw up as a TIFF chose their own, so a list of
the ones known today is a list that leaves tomorrow's raw undated. BigTIFF
widens every count and offset from four bytes to eight, and a pointer inside one
is read at the width its own type declares rather than at the file's offset
width.
.PP
Six more wrap a TIFF block somewhere else and hand it to that same parser:
Canon CR3 and CRM bury one in
.IR moov/uuid/CMT2 ,
HEIF and AVIF store one as an item the
.I meta
box points at, JPEG XL puts one in a top\-level
.B Exif
box, PNG in an
.B eXIf
chunk and WebP in an
.B EXIF
chunk, Fujifilm RAF gives the offset of a complete JPEG in bytes
84\-87 of its header, and Minolta MRW wraps one in a
.B \(rs0TTW
block.
.PP
The rest keep a date of their own making. Canon CRW holds a Unix timestamp in
the CIFF heap its trailing pointer leads to and Sigma X3F keeps one as a
.B TIME
property in a UTF\-16 property list; AVI writes the recording date out in words
in an
.B IDIT
chunk; Matroska counts nanoseconds from 2001 and ASF ten\-millionths of a second
from 1601; DV writes the date and time as binary\-coded decimal in the auxiliary
packs of its first blocks; and Redcode keeps one run of digits in a numbered
record. Every one is read as the camera's own clock and checked for a plausible
year, so a misread yields no date rather than a wrong one. Anything else on the
card, including AVCHD
.B .CPI
sidecars, is left untouched.
.PP
Storing no date this can read: AVCHD clips
.RB ( MTS ", " M2TS ", " M2T ),
MPEG program streams
.RB ( MPG ", " VOB ", " M2V )
and HLG photos
.RB ( HSP ).
Neither this nor exiftool finds a date inside them, so they are placed from a
sibling or the filesystem.
.B .TS
is not picked up at all, despite being the same transport stream as
.BR .MTS :
it holds no date either way, and a folder of TypeScript is the likelier
meaning.
.SH HOW THE DATE IS FOUND
The first of these that answers wins.
.IP 1. 4
.B EXIF
.IR DateTimeOriginal ", " CreateDate " or " ModifyDate ,
for every still and every raw listed above.
.IP 2. 4
.B The JPEG embedded in a raw
(tag 0x2e), for raws that carry the date nowhere else.
.IP 3. 4
.BR "A video clock that names its own timezone" :
the
.B \[co]day
user data Canon and Nikon write, Apple's
.IR com.apple.quicktime.creationdate ,
or the EXIF in the thumbnail Canon stores beside the clip. The local time is
taken as the camera's own clock and the offset discarded, that being the whole
point: it is what the photographer saw on the back of the camera.
.IP 4. 4
.B moov/mvhd
read verbatim, when the clip named no zone. The specification calls that field
UTC, but Panasonic writes the clock the camera is set to, so no conversion is
applied. Video is therefore filed exactly as stills are, by the camera's own
clock, and neither depends on the timezone of the machine doing the sorting.
.IP 5. 4
.BR "A sibling frame" :
the JPEG beside a raw of the same shot, or the THM beside a Canon AVI. Matched
on folder and file name, and failing that on file name alone, but only when
exactly one shot on the whole card goes by that name \- so a dual\-slot body
that split raw and JPEG across two cards still pairs up, while two cameras that
both wrote
.I DSC_0001
do not.
.IP 6. 4
.BR "The file's own timestamp" ,
for AVCHD clips and HLG photos, which hold no date this reads. Days containing
such files are marked
.B ~
in the summary.
.PP
Step 6 is only meaningful if whatever copied the card preserved the filesystem
dates.
.BR cp (1)
without
.B \-p
does not; it stamps every file with the moment of the copy. By default this is
detected \(em a filesystem date well after the newest date any file actually
records is the moment of a copy, not a shooting time \(em and those files are
placed in
.I undated/
instead of a wrong day. Copy the card with
.BR ditto (1),
.BR "cp \-p" ,
or
.BR "rsync \-a"
and they sort correctly;
.B \-\-use\-filesystem\-date
takes them as they are regardless.
.SH SAFETY
When one day holds two different photographs with the same file name, each goes
into a numbered subfolder of that day, oldest first:
.I 2026\-09\-01/01/A9999.RW2
for a frame shot at 10:00 and
.I 2026\-09\-01/02/A9999.RW2
for one shot at 22:00. This matters because a camera reuses file numbers when
its counter wraps and across
.I 100_PANA
and
.IR 101_PANA ,
so two different photographs really do arrive with the same name. A name that
clashes with nothing stays directly in the day folder, and the formats of one
shot stay together, since they differ by extension and so do not clash.
.PP
A destination name already taken by
.I identical
bytes means the file is already sorted, so it is skipped; under
.B \-\-move
the stray original is dropped
instead. That comparison reads both files in full rather than trusting
their size.
.PP
Under
.B \-\-move
a move within one filesystem is a rename; across filesystems the copy is
verified before the original is unlinked, and a short copy leaves the original
alone. Only folders that are already empty are removed, and never one named
on the command line.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B shotsort
Print the options and sort nothing.
.TP
.B shotsort \-n \-s ~/Import \-d ~/Pictures/2026
Print what that would do, and do none of it.
.TP
.B shotsort \-s ~/Import \-d ~/Pictures/2026
Read
.I ~/Import
and build the sorted day folders under
.IR ~/Pictures/2026 ,
leaving
.I ~/Import
exactly as it came off the card. This is the usual way to run it.
.TP
.B shotsort ~/Import
Sort it in place. The day folders hold copies and the originals stay in
.IR DCIM ,
so the folder briefly holds both.
.TP
.B shotsort \-m ~/Import
The same, moving the files rather than copying them, so nothing is duplicated
and the emptied
.I DCIM
folders are removed afterwards.
.TP
.B shotsort .
Sort the folder you are standing in.
.TP
.B shotsort \-\-layout '%Y/%F' ~/Import
Put a year level above the days.
.TP
.B shotsort \-\-day\-start 4 ~/Import
Keep a shoot that ran past midnight in the day it began.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TZ
Affects only files dated by step 6 above, whose filesystem dates the operating
system resolves in the local zone. Every file that records its own date, still
or video, is unaffected.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR exiftool (1),
.BR ditto (1),
.BR rsync (1),
.BR find (1)
.SH BUGS
AVCHD
.RB ( .MTS )
recording dates are stored in the transport stream itself and are not yet read;
such clips rely on step 5.
